# No em dashes in authored repo content

Em dashes (`—`, U+2014) and en dashes (`–`, U+2013) read as AI-generated,
render unpredictably in email clients and assistive tech, and break
visually on small screens. This is a fleet-wide rule: plain dash (`-`)
everywhere in content this repo's authors write, not just user-facing
copy. Use period, comma, semicolon, or colon instead of an em dash,
depending on the sentence shape. En dashes in numeric ranges (`10-20`)
become a plain dash too.

## Where this rule applies

- JSX / TSX text inside components and pages
- Form validation messages and yup / zod schemas
- Toast, banner, dialog, and inline error copy
- API route response bodies - anything in `error`, `message`,
  `description`, etc.
- Email templates (subject, plain-text, HTML) - `pact-notify/internal/templates/files/*`
- Backend error strings that bubble to the UI - e.g. `status.Error`,
  `status.Errorf`, `errors.New` returned by gRPC handlers when the
  Connect code is `InvalidArgument` (pact-web surfaces these via
  `err.rawMessage`)
- Any string an end user can read
- Code comments
- Test names, `t.Skip` / `t.Errorf` messages, `describe` / `it` labels
- Internal docs (`.cursor/rules/`, `.claude/`, `.agents/`, `AGENTS.md`,
  README files, and this file's own prose)
- Makefile and shell-script echoes, help text, and CLI output
- CI annotation strings (`::error::...`)
- Commit messages and PR bodies

## Where this rule does NOT apply

- Generated code (`src/__codegen__/**`, orval/buf output, and anything
  else produced by a codegen script rather than typed by a contributor)
  and `CHANGELOG.md`, which is auto-generated and must never be
  hand-edited
- Vendored/mirrored files pulled from another repo (e.g.
  `schema/**/swagger.yaml`, downloaded by `scripts/api-download.mjs`)
- Quoted external content or test fixtures where the assertion depends
  on byte-exact reproduction of input that isn't ours to rewrite
- CI annotation strings that echo external tool output verbatim
- This file's own illustrative uses of the characters: the diff example
  block below (whose em dashes are the pattern being illustrated -
  rewriting them would make the before/after lines identical), the
  Pattern column of the replacement guide, and the backtick character
  references naming `—` and `–` in the opening paragraph

## Replacement guide

| Pattern | Replace with | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Sentence break: `A — B.` | `A. B.` (capitalize B) | Two thoughts, two sentences |
| Tight aside: `X, Y — Z` | `X, Y, Z` (comma) | Aside is light enough |
| Apposition: `name — definition` | `name: definition` | Colon introduces a definition |
| Recovery instruction: `bad state — do X` | `bad state; do X` or `bad state. Do X.` | Semicolon for tight pairs |
| Numeric range: `10–20` | `10-20` | Plain dash, no special en dash |

## Examples (from this codebase)

These illustrative diffs intentionally keep their em dashes on the
`-` side, to show the exact character being replaced:

```diff
- Save these now — we'll only show them once.
+ Save these now. We'll only show them once.

- this is your only sign-in method — add a passkey before disconnecting
+ this is your only sign-in method; add a passkey before disconnecting

- password has appeared in a data breach — please choose a different one
+ password has appeared in a data breach. Please choose a different one

- If you didn't request a reset, ignore this message — your password hasn't changed.
+ If you didn't request a reset, ignore this message. Your password hasn't changed.
```

## Quick check before merging

```bash
# Repo-wide, this should return nothing. Generated/vendored content and
# this rule file's own three copies are excluded, since their
# illustrative examples are exempt (see above):
git grep -P '\xe2\x80\x94|\xe2\x80\x93' -- . \
  ':!CHANGELOG.md' \
  ':!pnpm-lock.yaml' \
  ':!src/__codegen__' \
  ':!schema/*/swagger.yaml' \
  ':!.cursor/rules/no-em-dash-in-user-text.mdc' \
  ':!.claude/rules/no-em-dash-in-user-text.mdc' \
  ':!.agents/rules/no-em-dash-in-user-text.mdc'
```
